895:
(A) Definitions.
Any collection of water as used in this section shall include, but shall not be limited to, those contained in ditches, pools, ponds, streams, excavations, holes, depressions, open cess pools and/or septic tanks, privy vaults, fountains, cisterns, tanks, shallow wells, barrels, troughs, urns, cans, boxes, bottles, tubes, appliances, automobiles and parts, buckets, roof gutters, tanks of flush closets, reservoirs, vessels, receptacles of any kind, or other container or devices which may hold water.
"Larvae" shall mean the immature stages of mosquitoes living in water between the egg stage and the pupa stage.
"Pupae" shall mean the immature stages of mosquitoes living in water and is the last stage before the adult form.
"Larvicide" shall mean the practice of killing mosquito larvae with stomach poisons, contact poisons or petroleum oils.
"Rodent infestation" shall mean the observation of live rats, droppings, runways, tracks, burrows, nests and gnawings, singly or in combination.
"Rodenticides" shall mean the practice of killing rodents with poisons, most of which are mixed with baits.
"Rodent" shall mean for the purpose of this section those members of the rodent family referred to as rats and mice.
(B) Prohibited acts.
(1) It shall be unlawful to have, keep, maintain, cause or permit, within the limits of Columbus, Georgia any collection of standing or flowing water in which mosquitoes breed or are likely to breed, unless such collection of water is treated so as effectually to prevent such breeding.
(2) It shall be unlawful to have, keep, maintain, cause or permit, within the limits of Columbus, Georgia any rodent infestation.
(C) Prevention and control.
(1) The methods used, or treatment directed toward the prevention of breeding mosquitoes in collections of water and for the prevention and treatment of rodents shall be of a type approved by the Columbus Department of Public Health. Approved methods of mosquito and rodent prevention and control may consist of one or more of the following procedures:
(a) Mosquito control.
(1) Remove from premises, vacant property, and areas all cans, boxes, broken or empty bottles, vehicle tires and all similar articles likely to hold water;
(2) Eliminate collections of water by filling or draining to the satisfaction of the health officer or his representatives;
(3) Larvicide collections of water by spraying with oil or otherwise treating the collected water with an effective chemical, and in a manner approved by the health department;
(4) Free collections of water of all vegetative growth, floating debris, and other obstructions prior and in addition to other control measures as may be required to prevent mosquito breeding;
(5) Stock collections of water with mosquito-destroying fish;
(6) Empty collections of water every seven days; Screen collections of water with corrosion resistant netting of at least 16 meshes to the inch each way; or with other material which will effectually prevent the ingress, egress of mosquitos;
(7) Such other methods as may be prescribed by the health department for special situations not covered above.
(b) Rodent control.
(1) Sanitate premises by maintaining good garbage storage in accordance with the Code of Ordinances of Columbus, Georgia, Article VII, Solid Waste Storage, Collection and Disposal;
(2) Eliminate the accumulation of rubbish, leaves and grass clippings which provide rodent harborage; store firewood, lumber or other materials 12 to 18 inches off the floor or ground;
(3) Store all foodstuffs both for human consumption and for animal consumption in rodent-proof containers 12 to 18 inches off the floor;
(4) Rodent-proof all openings into buildings to prevent entry of rodents;
(5) Treat rodent infestations with an effective rodenticide following manufacturer's directions, and in a manner approved by the health department;
(6) Use traps if poisons fail or are too risky;
(7) Such other methods as may be prescribed by the health department for special situations not covered above.
(D) Evidence and violation.
(1) The presence of mosquito larvae or pupae in collections of water shall be evidence that mosquitoes are breeding there, and failure to prevent such breeding within five days after notice by the health officer, his authorized agent, or representative, shall be deemed a violation of this section.
(2) The presence of live rats or mice, droppings, runways, tracks, burrows, nests and gnawings, singly or in combination shall be evidence that a rodent infestation does exist, and failure to prevent such rodent infestation within five days after notice by the health officer, his authorized agent, or representative, shall be deemed a violation of this section.
(E) Corrective action nonresidents. Should the property owner responsible for conditions giving rise to the breeding of mosquitoes or permits a rodent-infestation to exist, be a nonresident of Columbus, Georgia with no agent or representative in Columbus, Georgia, and said owner cannot be served with notice or summons, so as to subject him to recorder's court the health officer, his authorized agent, or representative shall notify such owner by mail, directing that the above stated conditions exists on his premises and that such conditions be corrected so as to comply herewith. If within ten days from the date of mailing the notice the owner fails to comply with the notice and this article, the health officer, his authorized agent, or representative shall correct and/or treat the mosquito breeding or rodent infestation, charging the total amount of the cost thereof to the owner, and such amount shall constitute a lien against the lot, as of the date of said work and if such amount is not paid within 30 days after completion of the work, the director of the department of finance shall issue execution against the particular real estate for which the service was rendered, and against the owner thereof, whereupon such real estate shall be levied on, advertised and sold under such execution, as in cases of levy and sale under executions for street improvement assessments due Columbus, Georgia with same procedure, including defendant's right to defense by affidavit of illegality and the right of Columbus, Georgia to purchase at the sale. No property shall be sold under the provisions of this section unless said sale is approved by resolution of the Council of Columbus, Georgia.
(F) Inspection of premises. For the purpose of enforcing the provisions of this section, the health officer, his authorized agent or representative, may at all reasonable times enter in and upon any premises within his jurisdiction, to ascertain whether the provisions of this section or any other ordinance have been complied with; and in case it should be ascertained that the provisions of this section or other ordinances are not being complied with, he shall notify the owner of such premises, the owner's agent or tenants to correct the violations and to make them conform with the requirements of this section or other ordinances.
(G) Enforcement. It shall be the duty of the Columbus Department of Public Health to see that provisions of this section are enforced and such department shall endeavor to secure all proper evidence and issue summons to defendants and witnesses in order that the recorder may be furnished with all essential and available facts upon the trial of offenses under this section.
(H) Penalties. Any person violating the provisions of this section shall be punished as set forth in section 1-8 of this Code.
(I) Partial invalidity clause. If any section, subsection, paragraph, sentence, clause or phrase of this section or the application of same to any particular set of persons or circumstances, should, for any reason, be held invalid, such invalidity all in no way affect the remaining portions of the section, and to such end the various provisions of this section are declared to be severable.
(Ord. No. 72-177, §§ 1, 2, 9-12-72)
Editor's note— Ord. No. 72-177, §§ 1 and 2, amended this Code by repealing former § 13-112 relative to the accumulation of water in open vessels in such a manner as to contribute to mosquito breeding, and enacted in lieu thereof a new § 13-112 as herein set out. Former § 13-112 was derived from Code 1914, § 734.

